  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1208 AM CDT Tue Oct 20 2026\n\nWV imagery indicates a west-southwest flow aloft prevailing across \nthe Western High Plains. Near the surface, a weak area of low pressure\nremains anchored in southeast Colorado.\n\nDespite a relatively dry pattern persisting early in the period, \nthere are minimal chances (20-40%) for showers and possibly an \nisolated thunderstorm here or there late today/early Wednesday as \nthe SREF points to another upper level shortwave trough lifting \nnortheast through the Southern Rockies early this morning, and \nfarther into the high plains of eastern Colorado this afternoon. \nNear the surface, modest moisture return at best will push surface \ndewpoints up into 50s(F) across southwest/south central Kansas, \nintroducing some meager instability with MUCAPE values pushing 500\nJ/kg or so by late afternoon. Meanwhile, an attendant frontal \nboundary to the organizing low in southeast Colorado is projected\nto develop/extend east-northeast through western Kansas by early\nevening. Although dynamic support aloft will remain less than \nfavorable, steepening low/mid-level lapse rates combined with \nvery marginal instability will set the stage for shower/isolated\nthunderstorm development across central Kansas and portions of \nsouthwest Kansas as H5 vort maxima transition through the high \nplains, interacting with the aforementioned boundary. The best \nchance for isolated showers/storm or two initially will be across\nextreme southwest Kansas late afternoon/early evening as suggested\nby some CAMs and the HREF indicating a 20-30% probability of 6-hr\nQPF exceeding a mere 0.1 of an inch by late evening with that \nprobability then shifting to central Kansas overnight. What little\nprecip chances exist dissipate Wednesday as a developing upper \nlevel ridge amplifies across the Western High Plains.\n\nAn ongoing warming trend continues today as prevailing southerlies \nhelp draw slightly warmer air into the area, pushing H85 temperatures\nwell above 10C in central Kansas to near 17-18C in extreme southwest\nKansas. With the HREF showing a 40-60% probability of temperatures\nexceeding 70F in central/south central Kansas to better than a 90%\nprobability of topping 70F elsewhere, expect widespread afternoon\nhighs in the 70s(F). Look for similar highs Wednesday.",
